    "summary": "High‑energy Dark Star → Cumberland and soaring Bird Song",
    "blurb": "The set opens with a powerful Morning Dew and builds through tight jams that peak with an epic Dark Star → Cumberland sequence. Jerry’s guitar is blistering throughout, while Keith adds jazz‑savvy keyboards to lift the improvisations. Bird Song and Playing In The Band close the first set on a high note.",
    "review": "September 27, 1972 finds the Dead at the Stanley Theatre delivering one of those rare nights where every musical thread seems perfectly tuned. After an opening Morning Dew that immediately grabs attention, the band rolls through classics like Friend Of The Devil and Black Throated Wind before launching into a Bird Song that feels both expansive and intimate. The first set closes with a soaring Playing In The Band, leaving the audience buzzing for what comes next.\n\nThe second set is anchored by a monumental Dark Star → Cumberland Blues journey; Jerry’s lightning‑fast leads weave through Keith’s jazzy chordal colors while Phil lays down a fluid, swinging bass foundation. Even with a slip on the Casey Jones solo, the overall performance remains tight and inspired, showcasing the group at the height of their early‑’70s chemistry. This soundboard capture by Vernon preserves every nuance, making it an essential listen for anyone wanting to experience the band’s high‑energy groove in crystal‑clear detail.",
